    {
      "id": "rj02faExQWmoL6XG1YHeE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1231.7545385267517,
      "y": 120.48143104713199,
      "width": 1155,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "MhsPHisMaDV5W6EpDf0Ir"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b09",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 383138857,
      "version": 2012,
      "versionNonce": 1326241449,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1742848258482,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Example when User is making the API call.\nTrack API is going to for each track or for a batch\nof tacks will get the routes of truck is going to.\nMiddleware is Auth Service - when user login and send \nGET /truck the middleware Auth will check if the\nuser is actually logged in or not, if is we're going to inject the user metadata\ninto the context. We will have a handler that will have user data like\nUser ID, his first Name, E-mail, all of that - only possible using context\n(middleware - context). \n\n* The same context may be passed to functions running in different goroutines, \ncontexts are safe for simultaneous use by multiple goroutines.\n! Important for us because the handlers on an HTTP server are usually a goroutine.\n\nAnother example is when user making calls to GET trucks and this calls the server Route\nand the server Route is maybe down so user will wait some time for response.\nTo user not to wait and check for response quite some time, we will use a Timeout()\nwhich will set for operation some time to exec(6s) and if there is not response \nwe will canceled. With go is very easy to use.\n\n! Context - is has a box that carries contextual information about request. So the power of context is to\ncarry information and ability to control the flow around the functions. \n\nInitializing context: context.Background() or context.TODO()\n\n! Context is immutable - cannot change or modify same.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Example when User is making the API call.\nTrack API is going to for each track or for a batch\nof tacks will get the routes of truck is going to.\nMiddleware is Auth Service - when user login and send \nGET /truck the middleware Auth will check if the\nuser is actually logged in or not, if is we're going to inject the user metadata\ninto the context. We will have a handler that will have user data like\nUser ID, his first Name, E-mail, all of that - only possible using context\n(middleware - context). \n\n* The same context may be passed to functions running in different goroutines, \ncontexts are safe for simultaneous use by multiple goroutines.\n! Important for us because the handlers on an HTTP server are usually a goroutine.\n\nAnother example is when user making calls to GET trucks and this calls the server Route\nand the server Route is maybe down so user will wait some time for response.\nTo user not to wait and check for response quite some time, we will use a Timeout()\nwhich will set for operation some time to exec(6s) and if there is not response \nwe will canceled. With go is very easy to use.\n\n! Context - is has a box that carries contextual information about request. So the power of context is to\ncarry information and ability to control the flow around the functions. \n\nInitializing context: context.Background() or context.TODO()\n\n! Context is immutable - cannot change or modify same.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
